Subject: [PATCH] present an EDR (half-float) resident instead of refusing it

macOS composites its desktop into an `R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT` framebuffer on this
platform. The window's present gate required BGRA byte order, so it refused that
resident and fell back to gathering the frame from the plane's guest pages --
which the resident rail deliberately never writes.

Measured with the strict gate: `direct_frac=0.00` for an entire boot, and
`window_publish ... fresh=0 no_frame=238`, i.e. the guest renders and nothing
reaches the screen.

Channel order only has to match for a path that copies bytes. The present blits,
and `cmd_blit_image` converts between colour formats and maps components by
meaning rather than byte position, so a non-BGRA resident is still presentable.
Formats outside the two float families still decline.

---
diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu-vulkan/src/pixel.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu-vulkan/src/pixel.rs
index eb165ef..404fa90 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu-vulkan/src/pixel.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu-vulkan/src/pixel.rs
@@ -682,6 +682,22 @@ pub fn has_bgra_order(format: vk::Format) -> bool {
     matches!(texel_layout_of(format), Some(TexelLayout::Bgra8))
 }
 
+/// Whether `format` is the RGBA half-float family.
+///
+/// Asked by the window's present gate, which used to require BGRA byte order.
+/// It does not have to: the present blits, and `cmd_blit_image` converts between
+/// colour formats and maps components by meaning rather than byte position. The
+/// format that made this necessary is the one macOS composites its desktop into
+/// on this platform -- an EDR `R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT` scanout, refused by the byte
+/// order rule and then never presented from the resident at all.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn is_rgba_float(format: vk::Format) -> bool {
+    matches!(
+        format,
+        vk::Format::R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT | vk::Format::R32G32B32A32_SFLOAT
+    )
+}
+
 /// Every Vulkan format a colour attachment may take, and the decline for a
 /// format the rail does not render to.
 ///
diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/vulkan/engine/pools/mod.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/vulkan/engine/pools/mod.rs
index 3ff3ce3..d7e70b2 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/vulkan/engine/pools/mod.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/vulkan/engine/pools/mod.rs
@@ -1813,7 +1813,21 @@ pub(crate) fn slot_present_decline(
     if !slot.content_ready {
         return Some(ResidentPresentDecline::ContentNotReady);
     }
-    if !slot.scanout_order() {
+    // Channel order only has to match for a path that copies bytes. The window
+    // presents with `cmd_blit_image`, which converts between colour formats and
+    // maps components by meaning (R to R, not by byte position), so a resident
+    // that is not in BGRA byte order is still presentable.
+    //
+    // This matters because macOS composites the desktop into an EDR framebuffer:
+    // the refused resident is `R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT` at the full 1920x1080. With
+    // the strict gate the window never took a resident at all (`direct_frac=0.00`
+    // for an entire boot) and fell back to gathering the frame from the plane's
+    // guest pages -- which the resident rail deliberately never writes. That is
+    // the stale-row striping: the display was reading a buffer nothing updates.
+    //
+    // Formats outside these two families still decline, because nothing has
+    // shown the blit does the right thing for them.
+    if !slot.scanout_order() && !translate::pixel::is_rgba_float(slot.format.declared()) {
         return Some(ResidentPresentDecline::ScanoutOrder);
     }
     if slot.width != width || slot.height != height {
